AxiomOS / Justicar

An operating system where the ethics are the infrastructure.

AxiomOS is a constitutional AI system: governance, privacy, and trust are enforced at the boundary every action must cross — not bolted on at the application layer. Most systems treat ethics as policy: rules that can be edited away or routed around. AxiomOS treats them as structure — constraints every action passes through by construction.

The constitutional core is implemented and running on local, consumer hardware. This repo holds the working code and the architecture it's growing into.

What works today

A working governance stack, running locally on open models (via Ollama) — no cloud dependency, nothing leaves the machine unless an action is explicitly and constitutionally permitted to send it:

Constitutional gate — every action an agent attempts is judged against the constitution before it runs. A deterministic rule layer hard-blocks known-dangerous operations; a model layer judges the ambiguous ones. It fails closed: if a decision can't be reached, the action is denied, not allowed.
Tamper-evident ledger — every decision (allowed or blocked, with its reasoning) is written to a hash-chained, append-only audit log. Any edit, deletion, or reorder of past entries is detectable by verifying the chain.
Persistent memory / knowledge graph — a local SQLite knowledge graph (entities + typed relations) with full-text recall, so the system accumulates and queries what it has learned across sessions instead of starting cold each time.
Governed agentic loop — a natural-language agent that acts through tools, where every tool call is routed through the gate and logged. There is no unobserved action.
Scope / authorization enforcement — actions against external targets are checked against an authoritative allow-list before execution; anything out of scope is refused at the gate, not trusted to the tool.
Running it

Requires Python 3 and Ollama with a local model pulled. The core modules are standalone and ship with self-contained demos:

bash
python3 axiom_gate.py      # watch the gate allow and deny sample actions
python3 axiom_ledger.py    # build an audit chain and verify its integrity
python3 memory.py          # store facts and relations, recall them, traverse the graph
python3 axiom_agent.py     # a governed natural-language agent (every tool call gated + logged)

(Adjust filenames to match your tree.)

The problem

Current AI infrastructure is centralized, extractive, and ungovernable by the people it affects. Privacy lives in policies that can change overnight. Educational tools surveil the learners they claim to serve. Platforms optimize for engagement over wellbeing. Governance gets captured by whoever has the most money. And underserved communities have no real access to any of it.

AxiomOS addresses these at the architectural level — not with better promises, but with structural constraints that can't be quietly removed.

Why kernel-level

Application-layer governance is a bouncer outside a club: anyone who doesn't want to follow the rules uses a different door. Kernel-level governance is the building itself — there is no other door. Every process crosses the kernel boundary to do anything meaningful, so enforcing the constitution at that chokepoint means no application can route around it. Not for complexity — for inescapability.

The architecture

Six components, in dependency order:

Component	What it does	Status
Constitutional Layer	The rules everything inherits; enforcement at the boundary.	Implemented
Natural Language Shell	Plain English as the primary interface — describe what you want.	Working prototype
Dual Data Lake	Separated public/private data; private lake gated by a hardware key.	Specified
Agora	Federated deliberation and governance; discovery over engagement.	Specified
Mastery Engine	Adaptive education that serves the learner and is barred from surveilling them.	Specified
Axiom Coin	Non-transferable contribution tracking; a trust cap that blocks governance capture by money.	Specified

Designed to run over an 802.11ah (HaLow) mesh so it doesn't depend on centralized internet infrastructure. Detailed design notes live alongside this README (kernel architecture, extended architecture, mesh layer, constitution draft).

Status & roadmap

The constitutional core — gate, audit ledger, memory, and governed execution — is implemented and running on consumer hardware. The remaining components are specified and on the roadmap.

Phase 1 — Proof of concept: the governance core (working) plus the Mastery Engine as the first full application.
Phase 2 — Network scale.
Phase 3 — Global distribution.

Built by a self-taught systems architect, from the architecture up.

